course: adult-life
lesson: 20
title: Going to the doctor and the chemist
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When grown-ups are ill, they usually see the GP, the family doctor at the local surgery. Doctors have more patients than time, so getting an appointment can mean ringing early or waiting weeks.
explain: The doctor may write a prescription, the written permission for a medicine you cannot just buy, and the pharmacist at the chemist hands it over.
explain: In Manchester, Mum sits with the phone at 7.59, ready to ring the surgery the second it opens. Grandma's pill box with the days of the week sits by the kettle, and the medicine cupboard is up high where little hands can't reach.
explain: Not every illness needs medicine: a cold gets better on its own. Jabs and check-ups keep people well before they get ill. In an emergency you ring 999, not the GP.
fact: The GP is the family doctor at the local surgery, and many surgeries give out the day's appointments first thing in the morning.
fact: For an emergency, such as someone not breathing or badly hurt, you call 999 (911 in the US and Canada), not the GP.
fact: Non-urgent appointments can be weeks away because doctors have more patients than time; urgent problems are seen sooner.
fact: A waiting list is a queue for a non-urgent operation, and in the NHS the wait can be months.
fact: A prescription is the doctor's written permission for a medicine that cannot be bought off the shelf; a pharmacist hands it over.
fact: Medicine prescribed for one person is only for that person.
fact: A repeat prescription is for medicine someone takes regularly, so they do not need to see the doctor every time.
fact: Many adults take a tablet every day to keep a long-term condition, like high blood pressure, under control.
fact: Medicines are kept high up or locked because they can be dangerous for children, and some look like sweets.
fact: An annual check-up is a routine visit to catch problems early, even when you feel fine.
fact: A jab is a vaccine given by injection; it teaches the body to fight a disease, and a booster tops up the protection.
fact: The flu jab is free for the over-sixty-fives because flu is much more dangerous for older people.
fact: Antibiotics kill bacteria but do nothing against colds, which are caused by viruses.
fact: A pill box with the days of the week helps someone see whether today's tablets have been taken and avoid a double dose.
exercise: Mum rings the GP at eight o'clock sharp. Why? | The day's appointments go fast first thing | The GP is only awake at eight o'clock in the morning | It is the cheapest time to phone | The surgery closes at five past eight
exercise: Who is the GP? | The family doctor at the local surgery | A surgeon who does big operations at the hospital | The chemist who sells medicine | A nurse who visits schools
exercise: Someone at home is not breathing. Who do you call? | 999, the emergency number | The GP's surgery in the morning | The chemist down the road | A friend from school
exercise: Dad sighs, "The earliest appointment is in three weeks." Why so long? | Doctors have more patients than time | The doctor is on holiday for three weeks in the sun | Dad asked for a date far away | Appointments are only given once a month
exercise: "He's on a waiting list for his knee operation." What does it mean? | He is queuing his turn for a non-urgent operation | He has had the operation already | He is waiting for a new pair of knees to arrive by post | He is waiting for a doctor to phone him back tonight
exercise: What is the printed slip Mum takes from the doctor to the chemist? | A prescription for medicine | A bill to pay for the doctor's visit | A note to take in for the school | A receipt for the hospital car park
exercise: Your friend has a prescription for an ear infection. Can you take some of her medicine for yours? | No, prescribed medicine is only for that person | Yes, if you both have sore ears at the same time | Yes, if you take only half the dose | Yes, if her mum says it is fine
exercise: "Can you pick up my repeat prescription?" What is a repeat prescription? | Regular medicine collected without a new appointment | Medicine you must take twice in one day, morning and night | A prescription the doctor wrote by mistake | A second copy in case the first is lost
exercise: Mum takes a small tablet every morning with her tea. What is the most likely reason? | To keep a long-term condition under control | Because she is addicted to tablets and can't stop taking them | To make her tea taste nicer | Because she has a cold every day
exercise: "Don't touch the medicine cupboard." Why? | Medicines can be dangerous for children | The cupboard door is broken and could fall off | Medicines go off if the door opens | It is where the sweets are kept
exercise: You find a tablet that looks like a sweet and swallow it by mistake. What do you do? | Tell a grown-up straight away | Wait and see if you start to feel ill | Drink lots of fizzy pop to wash it down | Keep it a secret so you are not told off
exercise: Dad says, "I've got to go for my annual check-up." Why go when he feels fine? | To catch any problems early | Because he is secretly very ill and hasn't told anyone | To get a note so he can skip work | Because he lost a bet with Mum
exercise: The school nurse says, "Time for your booster jab." What is a booster? | A jab that tops up protection from an earlier one | A jab that makes you grow taller | A jab that cures a cold you have now | A jab that gives you lots of extra energy for sport and games
exercise: Why is the flu jab free for the over-sixty-fives? | Flu is more dangerous for older people | Older people have more time to visit the surgery | The jab does not work on the young | Older people pay for it later in tax
exercise: The GP says, "It's only a cold. You don't need antibiotics." Why not? | Colds are caused by viruses, which antibiotics cannot kill | Antibiotics are only for grown-ups | The antibiotics have all run out | Colds are caused by bacteria that are too strong for any medicine at all
exercise: What does Grandma's pill box with the days of the week do? | Shows if today's tablets have been taken | Keeps her tablets warm in winter | Tells her which day of the week it is today | Holds sweets for the grandchildren
exercise: Why should you not open or move Grandma's pill box? | She needs it as it is to avoid mistakes | It is full of her secret jewellery and rings | The tablets go off in the light | It is an antique and very old
test: Overheard at 8.03: "Forty-third in the queue? You're joking. All gone already?" What happened? | The GP's appointments for today went quickly | The bus was full and drove past | The shop has run out of bread | The football tickets have sold out
test: Your brother says, "Just take some of Dad's pills for your headache, same thing." Put him right. | Medicine prescribed for Dad is only for Dad | He's right, all pills are the same inside | He's right if you take a small one | He's right because you're family
test: Nan in Brisbane rings: "Love, can you grab my scripts from the chemist, they're ready." What is she asking? | To collect her prescription medicines | To buy her some nice writing paper in town | To pick up her new glasses from the shop | To post her letters at the post office
test: Grandad, grumbling: "Eight months for a new hip! Might as well go private." What is going on? | He is on a long waiting list for an operation | He has already had his new hip | He is waiting for a new hip to be posted to him | The hospital has lost his hip X-ray
test: Your mate goes, "I've got a cold, my mum's getting me antibiotics, they fix everything." Put him right. | Antibiotics do nothing against cold viruses | He's right, antibiotics cure colds fast | He's right, they work on any illness | Only expensive antibiotics work on colds
test: Your little cousin is about to eat a pink tablet from Gran's handbag: "It's a sweetie!" What should happen? | Take it away and tell a grown-up at once | Let her, it is only one small tablet and can't hurt | Let her, if it is a pink one | Eat it yourself so she cannot
test: A Canadian boy sees his friend collapse and stop breathing. What should he do? | Call 911 and shout for a grown-up | Book an appointment with the doctor | Wait quietly until his friend wakes up | Go to the pharmacy to buy medicine
test: Mum, a bit embarrassed: "It's just my tablets, love, I take them every day." What is best? | Accept it; daily medicine is common and private | Hide the tablets so she cannot take them | Tell everyone at school about it | Take one to see what it does
test: Gran on the news: "Over-sixty-fives can now book their free flu jab." Why the free offer? | Flu is more dangerous for older people | Older people keep forgetting to pay | The jab only works on older people | Young people never catch the flu
test: Dad, putting it off again: "I feel fine, why'd I need a check-up?" What is the right answer? | Check-ups catch problems early, before you feel ill | He's right, check-ups are only for people who already feel ill | He's right, check-ups find nothing useful | Check-ups are only needed once in a lifetime
test: Your sister moans, "The booster's pointless, I already had that jab when I was little." Put her right. | A booster tops up the protection from the first jab | She's right, one jab lasts forever | She's right, boosters are only for adults | A booster is for a different disease entirely, not the old one
test: Grandma's pill box: Wednesday's lid is still shut at dinner time. What does it show? | She may have forgotten Wednesday's tablets | She has taken a double dose today | The box is broken and needs fixing | Wednesday's tablets are not needed
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a prescription? | A doctor's written authorisation for the supply of a medicine | A bill for the cost of a consultation | A certificate of absence from school | A record of a patient's height and weight
exam: Identify the principal reason for which antibiotics are not issued in respect of the common cold. | The common cold is caused by a virus, upon which antibiotics have no effect | The common cold is caused by bacteria resistant to all known forms of medicine | Antibiotics are reserved exclusively for persons over sixty-five | Antibiotics are prohibited for persons under eighteen
exam: In the event that a person has ceased breathing, which of the following courses of action is required? | The immediate summoning of the emergency services by telephone | The booking of an appointment with the general practitioner for the following week | The collection of a repeat prescription from the pharmacy | The awaiting of the person's spontaneous recovery
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a booster vaccination. | It augments the protection conferred by an earlier vaccination | It cures an infection already present | It renders all earlier vaccinations unnecessary | It is administered exclusively to the elderly
